</style></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to report</a><div class="shell"><header class="topline"><span class="brand">NATIVE SKETCH / ENGINEERING</span><span>TASK 002 · 14 OCT 2026</span><button class="print" onclick="window.print()" type="button">Print / save PDF</button></header><main id="main"><div class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Measured renderer comparison · macOS / Metal</div><h1>Skia vs Vello GPU.<br><span>A workload tradeoff.</span></h1><p class="lede">Skia is the stronger starting point for responsive drawing and navigation in this prototype. Vello GPU deserves to stay in contention for dense documents and heavy transparency.</p><span class="pill">Production renderer selection: OPEN</span><div class="recommend"><div class="eyebrow">Recommendation</div><h2>Use Skia for the next interactive integration experiment.</h2><p>Keep Vello GPU as an active candidate. First optimize the application’s scene caching and presentation schedule, then repeat the comparison before committing permanently. The current app’s prototype painters remain unchanged.</p></div><p class="note">Tested: Skia Ganesh/Metal (skia-safe 0.153.3) and Vello GPU Sparse Strips 0.3.0. This report compares pinned implementations on one Mac. It does not establish physical pen latency, displayed FPS, universal platform support, or the best performance either library can achieve.</p><p><a href="coregraphics-comparison.html">New: native AppKit/Core Graphics measurements and app-size audit</a></p><nav class="nav" aria-label="Report sections"><a href="#findings">Key findings</a><a href="#guarded">Guarded runs</a><a href="#matrix">Full matrix</a><a href="#tradeoffs">Pros &amp; cons</a><a href="#memory">Startup &amp; memory</a><a href="#quality">Visual quality</a><a href="#platforms">Platforms</a><a href="#method">Method &amp; evidence</a></nav></div>''')
sust=[p['guarded'] for s in replay['suites'] if s['suite']=='sustained' for p in s['pairs']]
primary=[p['guarded'] for s in replay['suites'] if s['suite']=='primary' for p in s['pairs']]
def values(runs,b): return [r['metrics']['service_without_acquire_ms']['p95'] for r in runs if r['backend']==b]
def cell(f,w=2560,m='paced'): return next(c for c in original['cells'] if c['fixture']==f and c['width']==w and c['mode']==m)
alpha=[r for r in primary if r['fixture']=='alpha' and r['mode']=='paced']
headlines=[('Guarded · 3 pairs × 180 s','Sustained pan & zoom',*[median(values(sust,b)) for b in colors],'1600×1000 · native-paced. Skia has lower submission cost; frame-start pacing still varies.'),('Original · 5 paired trials','5,000 retained strokes',*[stat(cell('dense5000'),b)['median'] for b in colors],'2560×1600 · native-paced. Vello has lower steady retained-scene cost. Original host caveat applies.'),('Guarded · 2 paired trials','Heavy transparency',*[median(values(alpha,b)) for b in colors],'2560×1600 · native-paced. Vello’s advantage persists after the host repair; this is one specific scene.')]
add('<section class="section" id="findings"><div class="eyebrow">01 / Decision signals</div><h2>Different workloads favor different engines.</h2><p>All headline values below are <strong>median process p95 service excluding drawable acquisition, in milliseconds</strong>. This removes a common drawable wait, but still includes internal backend waits. It is not pure CPU time or input latency.</p><div class="cards">')
for tag,title,s,v,note in headlines:
 add(f'<article class="card"><div class="eyebrow">{E(tag)}</div><h3>{E(title)}</h3><div class="numbers"><div class="s-text"><b>{s:.2f}</b><small>SKIA · ms</small></div><div class="v-text"><b>{v:.2f}</b><small>VELLO GPU · ms</small></div></div><p class="note">{E(note)}</p></article>')
add('''</div><p class="note">These cards use different workloads, conditions and repetition counts. They must not be averaged into an overall score. Product document limits, hardware budgets and acceptance targets remain undecided.</p><div class="two"><div><h3>Why the direction makes sense</h3><p>Skia retains vector paths and changes the canvas transform during navigation. This Vello GPU adapter reprocesses pixel-space strips when the transform changes. Live updates rebuild scene geometry under the same declared policy.</p></div><div><h3>Where the recommendation could change</h3><p>If large retained documents or layered transparency dominate the product workload, Vello’s advantages become more important. A separate live-ink overlay, transform caching, tiles and different presentation scheduling could change both engines’ results.</p></div></div></section>''')
add('''<section class="section" id="guarded"><div class="eyebrow">02 / Controlled replay</div><h2>The guarded runs confirm the main tradeoff.</h2><p>The repaired host records lifecycle state, holds activity assertions and uses a temporary floating window for automated trials. Full occlusion, minimization, session, sleep or surface changes invalidate a run. These controlled conditions are distinct from the original matrix and from a normal production window.</p><h3>Sustained pan &amp; zoom · every repetition</h3><p class="note">1600×1000 · native-paced · 180 measured seconds per process · source d473865. Bars show individual process p95 service excluding acquisition, without aggregation or confidence intervals.</p>'''+legend())
items=[]
for i in range(3):
 vals=[]
 for b in colors:
  r=next(r for r in sust if r['backend']==b and r['repeat_id']==f'sustained-{i}'); v=r['metrics']['service_without_acquire_ms']['p95']; vals.append((v,v,v))
 items.append((f'Repetition {i+1}',*vals))
add(chart(items,'Guarded sustained pan and zoom: service excluding acquisition p95','sust'))
rows=[]
for r in sorted(sust,key=lambda r:(r['repeat_id'],r['backend'])):
 rows.append([r['backend'].title()+' / '+r['repeat_id'][-1],f"{r['measured_frames']:,}",fmt(r['metrics']['service_without_acquire_ms']['p95']),fmt(r['cpu_frame_interval_ms']['p95']),f"{r['windows']['first_30s']['cpu_frame_interval_ms']['p95']:.2f} → {r['windows']['last_30s']['cpu_frame_interval_ms']['p95']:.2f}",f"{r['os_peak_rss_bytes']/2**20:.1f}"])
add(table(['Backend / repeat ID','Frames','Service p95, ms','Frame-start interval p95, ms','First → last 30 s interval p95, ms','Peak RSS, MiB'],rows,'Guarded sustained processes — exact values behind the chart (repeat IDs are zero-based)'))
add('''<p>Skia’s median process p95 frame-start interval is <strong>9.37 ms</strong>, versus <strong>25.77 ms</strong> for Vello. Skia’s individual interval p95 values range from 9.23 to 16.29 ms, so the evidence does not show uniformly stable display pacing.</p><div class="notice"><strong>The original sustained anomaly did not recur.</strong> One original Vello run slowed severely late in the trial; the guarded runs do not reproduce it. Its cause remains unknown. Missing callbacks cannot establish a display outage or assign blame to the renderer.</div><details><summary>All eight affected primary pairs: original versus guarded</summary><p class="note">Each row is one paired repetition, not a five-run aggregate. Original and guarded conditions differ; changes cannot be attributed solely to activity assertions or window level.</p>''')
pairs=[p for s in replay['suites'] if s['suite']=='primary' for p in s['pairs']]
keys=sorted({(r['fixture'],r['width'],r['mode'],r['repeat_id']) for r in primary})
rows=[]
for f,w,m,rid in keys:
 ps={p['guarded']['backend']:p for p in pairs if (p['guarded']['fixture'],p['guarded']['width'],p['guarded']['mode'],p['guarded']['repeat_id'])==(f,w,m,rid)}
 pair=lambda stage,metric: ' / '.join(fmt(ps[b][stage][metric]['p95']) for b in colors)
 rows.append([f'{names[f]} / {w} / {m} / {rid}',pair('original','cpu_frame_interval_ms'),pair('guarded','cpu_frame_interval_ms'),' / '.join(fmt(ps[b]['guarded']['metrics']['service_without_acquire_ms']['p95']) for b in colors)])
add(table(['Workload / width / mode / repeat','Original frame interval S / V, ms','Guarded frame interval S / V, ms','Guarded service S / V, ms'],rows,'Affected primary paired replays; S = Skia, V = Vello GPU'))
add('''</details><p class="note">All 16 primary and six sustained guarded processes passed lifecycle checks. Telemetry is still incomplete: 25 of 9,600 primary and one of 84,558 sustained presentation callbacks are missing. No displayed-FPS or deadline-miss rate is inferred. All 16 guarded primary final RGBA hashes match their original counterparts.</p></section>''')
add('''<section class="section" id="matrix"><div class="eyebrow">03 / Original experiment</div><h2>Explore all 32 workload conditions.</h2><p>Eight workloads × two resolutions × two scheduling modes. Each condition contains five fresh processes per backend; every process includes one first-use frame, 120 warm-up frames and 600 measured frames.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Original-host limitation.</strong> This matrix predates lifecycle guards. Nine primary trials had extended presentation callback gaps. All results are retained; the affected pairs were rerun separately above. Treat original display pacing and extreme magnitudes with caution.</div><p class="note">Charts show the median of five process p95 values; whiskers show their minimum–maximum range, not confidence intervals. Each chart has its own linear scale starting at zero. Tables include frame-start intervals and GPU timeline spans, which are different measurements and must not be summed.</p>''')
for w in [2560,1600]:
 for mode in ['paced','completion']:
  label=f'{w}×{1600 if w==2560 else 1000} · {"Native-paced (two frames in flight)" if mode=="paced" else "Completion (one frame in flight)"}'
  add(f'<details{ " open" if w==2560 and mode=="paced" else ""}><summary>{label}</summary><p class="note">Original source 32361fb · service excluding drawable acquisition p95 · milliseconds. Both modes present real native drawables; completion mode is not pure offscreen throughput.</p>'+legend())
  items=[]; rows=[]
  for f in order:
   c=cell(f,w,mode); vals=[]
   for b in colors:
    d=stat(c,b); vals.append((d['median'],d['min'],d['max']))
   items.append((names[f],*vals))
   rows.append([names[f],*[f"{stat(c,b)['median']:.2f} [{stat(c,b)['min']:.2f}–{stat(c,b)['max']:.2f}]" for b in colors], ' / '.join(fmt(stat(c,b,'cpu_frame_interval_ms')['median']) for b in colors),' / '.join(fmt(stat(c,b,'gpu_span_ms')['median']) for b in colors)])
  add('<h3>Drawing, navigation and retained content</h3>')
  add(chart([i for i in items if i[0] != names['alpha']],label+' — excluding transparency',f'matrix-{w}-{mode}'))
  add('<h3>Heavy transparency · separate axis</h3><p class="note">Transparency is shown separately so its large range does not compress the other workloads. Both charts retain every measured minimum–maximum range; compare the labeled values and axis scales.</p>')
  add(chart([i for i in items if i[0] == names['alpha']],label+' — transparency',f'alpha-{w}-{mode}'))
  add(table(['Workload','Skia service p95 [range], ms','Vello service p95 [range], ms','Frame interval S / V, ms','GPU span S / V, ms'],rows,label+' — median of five process p95 values'))
  add('</details>')
add('''<p>Across all five original high-resolution native-paced pairs, Vello’s service p95 exceeds Skia’s by <strong>22.38–27.54 ms for pan/zoom</strong> and <strong>1.00–1.82 ms for live drawing</strong>. For 5,000 retained strokes, Vello is lower by <strong>7.56–8.75 ms</strong>. Small, mixed light-scene differences should not drive selection.</p></section>''')
add('''<section class="section" id="tradeoffs"><div class="eyebrow">04 / Engineering judgment</div><h2>Pros, cons and integration costs.</h2><div class="two"><article class="card pros"><div class="eyebrow">Skia Ganesh / Metal</div><h3>Best next interaction baseline</h3><h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Lower live-update and pan/zoom service costs in this implementation; sustained pan/zoom direction survives the guarded replay.</li><li>Retained vector paths allow inexpensive transform changes under the current policy.</li><li>Lower first-use service and process peak RSS than Vello for the 5,000-stroke fixture.</li></ul><h4>Disadvantages and risks</h4><ul><li>Much higher costs for high-resolution overlapping transparency; the disadvantage persists under guarded conditions.</li><li>Higher steady rendering costs for the retained dense scene.</li><li>Native C++ binary/build dependency. The minimal Metal prebuilt archive returned 404; unused JPEG/PDF features were enabled to consume a published package.</li><li>Cache strategy and production surface lifecycle still need integration work.</li></ul></article><article class="card cons"><div class="eyebrow">Vello GPU / Sparse Strips</div><h3>Strong retained-scene candidate</h3><h4>Advantages</h4><ul><li>Lower steady costs for 5,000 retained strokes and high-resolution heavy transparency.</li><li>Substantially lower process RSS for the transparency fixture.</li><li>Rust/wgpu integration avoids the Skia C++ binary dependency, although it has its own resource and dependency constraints.</li></ul><h4>Disadvantages and risks</h4><ul><li>CPU strip reprocessing makes pan/zoom expensive with this adapter’s invalidation policy.</li><li>Live-update service costs are higher in the original paired trials.</li><li>Higher first-use service and peak RSS for the 5,000-stroke fixture despite lower steady costs.</li><li>Results apply to Sparse Strips 0.3.0, not every Vello variant or future caching strategy.</li></ul></article></div><p class="note">Packaging observations are concrete setup experience, not measured build-time or binary-size comparisons. Neither candidate has been independently optimized into a production drawing architecture.</p><h3>Recommended next experiment</h3><ol><li><strong>Keep the shared Rust document and editing semantics.</strong> Preserve native controls and put renderer-specific work behind a narrow canvas boundary.</li><li><strong>Integrate Skia in one scoped interactive prototype.</strong> Measure live ink and navigation with a retained base scene plus transient ink overlay.</li><li><strong>Give both engines equivalent optimized policies.</strong> Explore dirty regions/tiles, transform caching and preparing CPU work before drawable acquisition.</li><li><strong>Repeat on agreed workloads and hardware.</strong> Check tail timings, first use, memory, visual quality and observable presentation. Add physical input-to-screen measurements before making latency claims.</li><li><strong>Decide with the owner.</strong> Weight real document sizes and platform requirements before choosing a production renderer. No automatic winner score or permanent commitment is proposed here.</li></ol></section>''')
add('''<section class="section" id="memory"><div class="eyebrow">05 / Costs beyond steady rendering</div><h2>First use and memory change the picture.</h2><p>Vello’s retained dense-scene advantage comes with a higher first-use cost in this fixture. Heavy transparency is the opposite: Skia uses substantially more whole-process memory.</p><p class="note">Original 2560×1600 completion-mode medians over five fresh processes. First-use service excludes explicit GPU retirement. These are not guaranteed cold filesystem or shader-cache starts.</p>''')
rows=[]
for f in order:
 c=cell(f,2560,'completion')
 rows.append([names[f],*[' / '.join(f"{c['backends'][b][key]/div:.1f}" for b in colors) for key,div in [('init_ms_median',1),('first_use_service_ms_median',1),('os_peak_rss_bytes_median',2**20)]]])
add(table(['Workload','Initialization S / V, ms','First-use service S / V, ms','Process peak RSS S / V, MiB'],rows,'Startup and memory — S = Skia, V = Vello GPU'))
add('''<div class="notice"><strong>RSS is not isolated renderer memory.</strong> It includes the AppKit host, both linked libraries, common fixture/image data, retained measurement rows and untimed readback/PNG output. Faster sustained processes store more rows. Unified CPU/GPU memory counters must not be added together.</div><p class="note">Live initialization includes roughly one second of common geometry-identity precomputation. Finish-time Metal allocation is a snapshot, not peak residency. Physical footprint, GPU peak memory, energy consumption and final package size were not compared.</p></section>''')
add('''<section class="section quality" id="quality"><div class="eyebrow">06 / Output fidelity</div><h2>Geometry agrees; pixels are not identical.</h2><p>The designer reviewed all eight fixture pairs at both captured resolutions. No visible correctness blocker was found: placement, pressure widths, stroke order, translucent interiors, curved clipping and image orientation agreed. Edge coverage and faint image-filtering differences remain.</p><p class="note">Selected original 2560×1600, frame-599 comparison sheets below contain Skia and Vello outputs, an amplified RGB difference and descriptive statistics. They are embedded in this HTML and remain available offline. Difference magnification is 8×; pixel error is not an acceptance threshold.</p>''')
for f in ['live','alpha','image']:
 img=DATA/'frame599-2560'/f'{f}-comparison.png'
 encoded=base64.b64encode(img.read_bytes()).decode()
 add(f'<details><summary>{names[f]} — matched output and amplified differences</summary><figure><img loading="lazy" width="1600" src="data:image/png;base64,{encoded}" alt="Comparison sheet for {names[f]}: labeled Skia and Vello output, amplified pixel difference and descriptive statistics. No visual blocker was found; coverage and filtering can differ."><figcaption>Original completion-mode repeat 0 · source 32361fb · physical render size 2560×1600. The sheet scales the outputs for viewing.</figcaption></figure></details>')
add('<p>Static captures do not establish temporal smoothness, every live-frame union, every clipping combination, source-image colorimetry or physical pen feel. Large originals were downsampled by the viewing tool during review; this was not exhaustive one-to-one pixel inspection.</p><p>'+source_link('docs/evidence/renderer-comparison/frame599-2560','All eight high-resolution comparison sheets')+' · '+source_link('docs/evidence/renderer-comparison/frame150-1600','All eight intermediate-frame comparison sheets')+'</p></section>')
add('''<section class="section" id="platforms"><div class="eyebrow">07 / Product implications</div><h2>One shared engine does not remove platform validation.</h2><p>The evidence is macOS/Metal only. The following are integration directions and open checks, not completed port certifications or claims that either pinned renderer supports every target.</p><div class="platform">''')
add(table(['Platform family','Native interface direction','What this comparison leaves open'],[
['macOS','Swift + AppKit; SwiftUI where control behavior fits','Both candidates exercised on one Mac. Production surface lifecycle, pen latency, energy and wider hardware remain open.'],
['iPhone / iPad / visionOS','Swift + UIKit / appropriate native SwiftUI surfaces','Target builds, touch/Pencil or spatial input, Metal surface integration, memory budgets and sustained device thermals.'],
['Apple TV / Apple Watch','Native viewer-focused interface; device-specific previews may be appropriate','Renderer feasibility and viewer policy must be checked per SDK. A desktop Metal result is not watchOS support evidence.'],
['Android / ChromeOS','Kotlin + Android Views / standard Material controls where supported','Android distribution/runtime availability, GPU drivers, stylus, mouse/keyboard and resizable desktop windows. ChromeOS product/OS targets need confirmation.'],
['Windows','C# + WinUI 3, calling shared Rust','Native composition/surface integration, GPU backend and driver validation, pen input and comparable measurements.'],
['Linux','Rust + GTK4 / libadwaita','Window-system integration, Vulkan/driver coverage, input and desktop-specific behavior.'],
['Browser','TypeScript + semantic HTML, Rust/Wasm canvas engine','Actual Wasm build/backend feasibility, WebGPU availability/fallbacks, browser input and package/startup cost.']
],'Planned native UI boundaries and required target evidence'))
add('''</div><p class="note">This report does not verify “GooglebookOS” as a specific OS/runtime target. Confirm the product’s actual platform and distribution model before assigning it an Android, browser or Linux implementation path. Native controls and shared Rust semantics remain separate from the canvas renderer choice.</p></section>''')
add('''<section class="section" id="method"><div class="eyebrow">08 / Trust and reproducibility</div><h2>What was tested, and what was not.</h2><div class="two"><div><h3>Exact candidates</h3><p><strong>Skia:</strong> skia-safe 0.153.3, Ganesh over Metal, upstream binding reference m153-0.101.2.</p><p><strong>Vello GPU:</strong> vello_gpu 0.3.0 + vello_common 0.3.0, Sparse Strips CPU path preprocessing with GPU rasterization/compositing. This is not compute-centric Vello 0.8.0 or Vello CPU.</p><p><strong>GPU access:</strong> wgpu 30.0.0, locked internals 30.0.1; Metal only.</p></div><div><h3>Reference environment</h3><p>Apple M2 Pro, 12 CPU cores, 16 GiB unified memory. macOS 27.0.1 (26A434), SDK 27.0, Rust 1.99.0, Swift 6.4. AC power; nominal 120 Hz display, backing scale 2.</p><p>Other desktop applications and OS services remained open. No concurrent agent builds, GPU experiments or native UI automation ran during measurements. Thermal observations recorded no warning; this does not prove absence of throttling.</p></div></div><h3>Equivalent inputs, explicit policy</h3><p>Both adapters consume real Rust Editor::render outlines, fill each whole stroke once with nonzero winding, and share geometry, traces, transforms and decoded image bytes. Both use the same Metal device/queue, BGRA8 offscreen target, final native blit and CAMetalLayer policy. There is no timed CPU readback.</p><p>Static vector resources are retained but rasterization occurs every frame. The common pipeline acquires a drawable <em>before</em> CPU scene preparation, so a CPU-heavy engine holds it during that work. Alternative overlap/scheduling was not tested. Both use analytic coverage at sample count 1, bilinear filtering without mipmaps, and numeric sRGB channels without linear-light conversion; this does not prove equal antialiasing quality.</p>''')
add(table(['Measurement','Interpretation'],[
['Service excluding acquisition','Per-frame service minus drawable acquisition. Includes synchronous backend waits; not pure CPU execution or input latency.'],
['CPU frame-start interval','Time between consecutive CPU frame starts. Includes retirement backpressure and scheduling; not displayed FPS.'],
['GPU timeline span','Common begin marker before scene preparation through final blit GPU end. Includes CPU/queue gaps; not pure GPU execution. Do not sum with overlapping metrics.'],
['p95 and variation','Nearest-rank process percentiles. Original chart medians summarize five process p95s, with min–max whiskers. No pooled-frame headline percentile or confidence interval.'],
['Presentation callbacks','Positive drawable presentedTime observations only. Gaps do not prove display outages or deadline misses.'],
['Unsupported conclusions','No physical pen-to-screen latency, true missed-display-deadline rate, GPU peak residency, energy, package-size or universal platform ranking.']
],'How to read the measurements'))
add('''<h3>Evidence coverage</h3><ul><li><strong>Original:</strong> 320 primary + six sustained processes; 262,354 measured frames with GPU timeline spans. Eighteen calibration and sixteen separate quality processes are additional.</li><li><strong>Guarded:</strong> 16 affected primary + six sustained processes; 94,158 GPU-timed measured frames. Valid complete pairs were assembled with source hashes. Failed/minimized attempts and an excluded unmatched run are preserved.</li><li><strong>Calibration:</strong> ordered marker-off and sampler-off pilots had variable signs and magnitudes. No causal overhead estimate or correction was applied.</li></ul><h3>Verification and independent review</h3><p>Integration revision <code>6e1fb271f68f6122108164ce515d871a59df9ed0</code> passed the repository and renderer checks: 26 production Rust tests, five experiment Rust tests, 24 Python analysis/recovery tests, native release build and Swift/Rust integration. The final interactive app rendered and restored its controls; this inspection is not performance data.</p><p>Independent reliability review reproduced original and guarded summaries and checked raw identities. A fresh reviewer’s lifecycle-observability finding led to the repair and separate reruns. Final evidence review found no unresolved actionable finding. Designer inspection covered native controls and matched output images.</p><p class="note">Native host keyboard-event delivery was inconclusive; minimum-width resizing and dark appearance remain unverified. Browser CI passed for the PR, but it does not certify native GPU results. This HTML presents existing evidence; it introduces no new benchmark runs.</p><h3>Provenance and source material</h3>''')
rows=[['Original measured source',original['source_revision']],['Guarded measured source',replay['guarded_source_revision']],['Evidence snapshot','65e7b333f127415dfc15887fbbe614d0ada3f7ee']]
for f in ['results.json','guarded-replay.json']: rows.append([f+' SHA-256',hashlib.sha256((DATA/f).read_bytes()).hexdigest()])
add('<div class="code">'+table(['Input','Revision or digest'],rows,'Report data identities')+'</div>')
